content.paragraph:
Since $A = PDP^{- 1}$, we have $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      T
      \end{array} \right\rbrack_{B} = P^{- 1}AP = D$.

example.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      4 & {- 9} \\
      4 & {- 8}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\textbf{b}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\textbf{b}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is $(\text{λ} + 2)^{2}$, but the eigenspace for the eigenvalue $- 2$ is only one-dimensional; so $A$ is not diagonalizable. However, the basis $B = \{\textbf{b}_{1},\textbf{b}_{2}\}$ has the property that the $B\text{-matrix}$ for the transformation $\textbf{x}\mapsto A\textbf{x}$ is a triangular matrix called the Jordan form of $A$.1 Find this $B\text{-matrix}$.

note.paragraph:
An efficient way to compute a $B\text{-matrix}$ $P^{- 1}AP$ is to compute $AP$ and then to row reduce the augmented matrix $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      P & {AP}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ to $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      I & {P^{- 1}AP}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. A separate computation of $P^{- 1}$ is unnecessary. See Exercise 22 in Section 2.2.

statement.paragraph:
Let $B = \{\textbf{b}_{1},\textbf{b}_{2},\textbf{b}_{3}\}$ be a basis for a vector space $V$. Find $T(3\textbf{b}_{1} - 4\textbf{b}_{2})$ when $T$ is a linear transformation from $V$ to $V$ whose matrix relative to $B$ is$$\lbrack T\rbrack_{B~} = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      0 & {- 6} & {~~~1} \\
      0 & {~~~5} & {- 1} \\
      1 & {- 2} & {~~~7}
      \end{bmatrix}$$

Let $B = \{\textbf{b}_{1},\textbf{b}_{2},\textbf{b}_{3}\}$ be a basis for a vector space $V$. Find $T(2\textbf{b}_{1} - \textbf{b}_{2} + 4\textbf{b}_{3})$ when $T$ is a linear transformation from $V$ to $V$ whose matrix relative to $B$ is$$\lbrack T\rbrack_{B~} = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      0 & {- 6} & {~~~1} \\
      0 & {~~~5} & {- 1} \\
      1 & {- 2} & {~~~7}
      \end{bmatrix}$$

Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 1 \\
      {- 1} & 3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $B = \{\textbf{b}_{1},\textbf{b}_{2}\}$, for $\textbf{b}_{1} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack,\textbf{b}_{2} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Define $T:\text{ℝ}^{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2}$ by $T(\textbf{x}) = A\textbf{x}$.

[T] Let $T$ be the transformation whose standard matrix is given below. Find a basis for $\text{ℝ}^{4}$ with the property that $\lbrack T\rbrack_{B}$ is diagonal.$$A~ = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      15 & {- 66} & {- 44} & {- 33} \\
      0 & {~~~13} & {~~~21} & {- 15} \\
      1 & {- 15} & {- 21} & {~~~12} \\
      2 & {- 18} & {- 22} & {~~~~~8}
      \end{bmatrix}$$
